    "description": "Iranian ceramics encompass architectural tiles and vessels, with historical production combining specialized bodies, glazes, and painted decoration. Kashan was an important medieval production center, while the modern industry developed around a broader domestic mineral resource base. [s1][s3]",
    "tags": ["Ceramics", "Architectural tiles", "Kashan", "Materials", "Craft history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Tiles & Ceramics in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian architectural ceramics, Kashan tilemaking, medieval glazes, and the evidence needed to understand today's industrial sector."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "materials-and-industry",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mineral resources to architectural surfaces",
        "content": "Iran's ceramic history connects material preparation with the appearance of buildings. A tile is both a manufactured object and part of an architectural composition; understanding its body, glaze, decoration, and placement reveals more than identifying its color alone. Iranica's industrial survey identifies domestic resources including clay, feldspar, kaolin, quartz, and mineral oxides as an important foundation for ceramic manufacture. That resource base helps explain the industry's development, but the survey's historical cost and import statements cannot establish present operating conditions. [s3]\n\nHistorical artistic ceramics and contemporary factory output also require different evidence. Signed objects, excavated fragments, and technical manuscripts illuminate earlier practice. Modern production needs dated industrial statistics with explicit product categories. Neither kind of evidence substitutes for the other. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kashan-and-technology",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Kashan and the importance of ceramic technology",
        "content": "Kashan, a city in central Iran, was a major center of medieval ceramic production. Signed vessels and tiles connect makers with the city, although scholars caution that many ceramic types were produced in more than one locality. A style therefore cannot always identify a workshop securely. Artists also traveled to make architectural ceramics elsewhere. [s1]\n\nAn important technological development was the use of fritware, a ceramic body containing glassy ingredients that supported fine bodies and compatible glazes. Innovations in glaze preparation enabled painted decoration beneath translucent surfaces. A technical account written in 1301 by Abu'l-Qasem, a member of a Kashan potting family, provides unusually direct evidence for this manufacturing knowledge. [s1]\n\nLuster painting produced brown or golden effects, while other approaches placed colored decoration beneath a clear glaze or added enamel decoration above it. These were distinct technical choices rather than interchangeable names for colorful pottery. Architectural tiles brought such knowledge into larger compositions, extending ceramic manufacture beyond portable vessels. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continuity-and-disruption",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Continuity, disruption, and changing designs",
        "content": "The effects of the Mongol invasions require careful wording. Iranica emphasizes continuity in ceramic techniques across the invasions, whereas the Ashmolean Museum describes severe disruption to manufacture and trade followed by fuller recovery later in the thirteenth century. These accounts emphasize different aspects: survival of technical traditions does not necessarily imply uninterrupted output. [s1][s2]\n\nThe Ashmolean identifies renewed luster production with a stronger emphasis on tiles. It also describes lotus, dragon, and phoenix motifs entering the decorative repertoire through wider Asian exchange. Its collection account distinguishes later overglaze enamel decoration from earlier minai wares and explains lajvardina, named after the Persian word for lapis lazuli, through its dark blue glazed ground. These connections place Iranian ceramics within trading and artistic networks rather than an isolated national tradition. [s2]\n\nFor the modern sector, the remaining task is quantitative: establish comparable production, trade, and regional manufacturing evidence. Historical artistic achievement alone cannot establish contemporary industrial scale or competitiveness.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
